From 494650c71e3356859dbb630fc87f35a226879788 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:44:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] edge, pushdown: harden near-duplicate roots and N1 after
 adversarial review

Near-duplicate roots (reviewer round 2, each reproduced by a probe):
- no chaining: a record joins only a record that is its own root, so B~A and
  C~B no longer merge C with A, and the result no longer depends on order;
- backward window only: an earlier-dated late record no longer joins a later
  record's root;
- a near-duplicate must carry exactly the same values (pure numbers such as
  3, 40%, 1.5) and the same negation words, so templated alerts with other
  readings, and a negated report beside the positive one, stay apart;
- candidates come from an index on (structured, received_date): one 50,000-
  record site ingests in 16.9 s with the rule on, 3.5 s off (was quadratic:
  81.6 s at 4,000).
Through the real EdgeSite ingest on dev seeds 0-5: 1 of 3,912 true-pattern
records changes root; echo groups collapse to 1.04-1.18 roots per group.
4 new regression tests fail on 7bbaad1d and pass here.

N1: the hypothesis reason no longer counts a <k site as one 'with at least
k records'; the support record names corroborating_sites when N1 is on (absent
under R0, so R0 output bytes are unchanged).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Cc9kurj5azoGuhPNUVde8U
